  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The manager of a bakery estimates that the profit of the bakery, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-2{x}^{2}+120x-1\\,500,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of loaves baked daily. Given that the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, how many loaves should the bakery bake per day to maximize its daily profit?\n\nThe bakery should bake\n{{field-1}} loaves per day to maximize its daily profit.",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x$ represents the number of loaves baked daily and the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 400.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<400.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-4x+120,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -4x+120 & =0 \\\\ x & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=30$ lies in the interval $0<x<400.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=30$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=30$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=30$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$\n\nTherefore, the bakery should bake $30$ loaves per day to maximize its daily profit.",
